      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54180,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 163,
        "headingText": "<p >Lymphoepithelioma-like carcinoma (8082/3)</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-02-07T18:21:09"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 364,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 164,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54181,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 164,
        "headingText": "<p>This carcinoma is most often located in the lower oesophagus or the oesophagogastric junction {25582499}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2018-12-17T14:15:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 365,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 165,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54182,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 165,
        "headingText": "<p>The most common symptom is progressive dysphagia, followed by gastro-oesophageal reflux, weight loss, and anaemia {25582499}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2018-12-17T14:15:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 366,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 166,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54183,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 166,
        "headingText": "<p>The reported relative prevalence varies widely, from 0.18% to 4% of oesophageal carcinomas {19196264; 17437008}; this apparent variation is probably related to the lack of diagnostic criteria. In reported US series, patients ranged in age from 39 to 84&nbsp;years (mean: 65.5&nbsp;years) and were predominantly male {25582499}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2018-12-17T14:15:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 367,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 167,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54184,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 167,
        "headingText": "<p>Unknown</p>",
        "timestamp": "2018-12-17T14:15:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 368,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 168,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54185,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 168,
        "headingText": "<p>Undifferentiated carcinoma most likely results from the dedifferentiation of squamous cell carcinoma or adenocarcinoma of oesophageal epithelial origin. In the largest study of undifferentiated carcinomas to date, 12&nbsp;of 16&nbsp;cases (75%) were associated with Barrett oesophagus, and some of those carcinomas contained focal glandular differentiation {25582499}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2018-12-17T14:15:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 375,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 207,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54186,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 207,
        "headingText": "<p>The tumours are exophytic, with raised edges, and they are centred in either the oesophagus or the oesophagogastric junction {25582499}. In most cases, there are central areas of depression and ulceration.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2018-12-17T14:16:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 369,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 169,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54187,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 169,
        "headingText": "<p>The tumour cells form variably sized nests and sheet-like arrangements. The cells are medium-sized to large, with poorly defined amphophilic to slightly eosinophilic cytoplasm imparting a syncytial-like appearance. Cytologically, the nuclei are oval and vesicular. Large pleomorphic nuclei and multinucleated giant cells mimicking osteoclasts or rhabdoid cells are occasionally found (see <a name=\"668\" class=\"attchClick\" style=\"cursor:pointer\" data-toggle=\"modal\" data-target=\"#viewierModal\">#668</a>) {25582499}.</p>\r\n<p>Lymphoepithelioma-like carcinoma {23573354} is considered to be a distinct subtype of undifferentiated carcinoma. Most reported cases have been from Japan and clinically resemble conventional squamous cell carcinoma {11577322; 18031397}. This subtype is characterized by a sheet-like arrangement of large epithelioid cells with prominent nucleoli and indistinct cell borders. The tumour is surrounded by a characteristic inflammatory infiltrate that is rich in lymphocytes and plasma cells (see <a name=\"607\" class=\"attchClick\" style=\"cursor:pointer\" data-toggle=\"modal\" data-target=\"#viewierModal\">#607</a>). Unlike similar tumours located in the head and neck region, lymphoepithelioma-like carcinoma of the oesophagus is only occasionally positive for EBV {23573354}.</p>\r\n<p>When present, cytokeratin positivity may help exclude non-epithelial neoplasms such as melanoma, lymphoma, and sarcoma. Other immunohistochemical markers are recommended for distinguishing undifferentiated carcinoma from neuroendocrine carcinoma (NEC; chromogranin&nbsp;A, synaptophysin, and CD56), poorly differentiated squamous cell carcinoma (p63 and p40), and poorly differentiated adenocarcinoma (mucins).</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-08-23T11:03:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 370,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 170,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54188,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 170,
        "headingText": "<p>Undifferentiated carcinoma consists of malignant cells without evidence of differentiation.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2018-12-17T14:16:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 371,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 171,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54189,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 171,
        "headingText": "<p>Not clinically relevant</p>",
        "timestamp": "2018-12-17T14:16:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 374,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 174,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54190,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 174,
        "headingText": "<p><em>Essential:</em> malignant cells without evidence of differentiation by histology; equivocal immunohistochemistry.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2018-12-17T14:16:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 372,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 172,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54191,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 172,
        "headingText": "<p>The staging of oesophageal undifferentiated carcinoma follows that of oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2018-12-17T14:17:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 373,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 173,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54192,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 173,
        "headingText": "<p>In a US series, most undifferentiated carcinomas were detected at an advanced stage; the prognosis was poor, with a 1-year survival rate of 25% {25582499}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2018-12-17T14:17:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]